<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Quick Look extension entitlements.

  An app extension gets its own sandbox rather than inheriting the
  parent's, so com.apple.security.inherit is wrong here: that key is for
  child processes of the app, and an .appex is launched by the system,
  not by us.

  Read-only file access is the whole grant. Quick Look hands the provider
  a URL for exactly the file being previewed; the extension never opens
  anything else, has no network access, and needs nothing further.

  The application identifier and team identifier are normally injected
  by Xcode from the provisioning profile. This extension is signed by
  hand against an explicit entitlements file, and that replaces the
  entitlement set rather than merging into it, so without these two the
  signature carries no identifier while the embedded profile does.
  Upload validation accepts that and then refuses the build for
  TestFlight, warning 90886, because the two must agree. Both profiles,
  development and distribution, declare the same identifier, so one
  value serves both builds.

  Watch the punctuation in this comment. codesign parses entitlements
  with AMFI, and XML forbids a doubled hyphen inside a comment, so
  writing a command line flag with its leading dashes here fails the
  whole file with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error" while plutil still
  reports it as valid.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
	<string>VN3M9RS6R3.com.manifestchess.lite.PGNPreview</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>VN3M9RS6R3</string>
</dict>
</plist>
